@startuml
' CSE 360 Team Project - TP1 State Diagram: Email Address Recognizer
' Author: A.G. (agupt545) - Design Documents and Architecture
' Version 1.00, 2026-09-17
' This is the directed graph implemented by emailAddressRecognizer.EmailAddressRecognizer,
' drawn in the same manner as the provided "Directed Graph - Password Evaluator" diagram.

title TP1 State Diagram - Email Address Recognizer

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontSize 11

[*] --> SizeCheck

SizeCheck : Check the size of the input
SizeCheck : BEFORE examining it
SizeCheck --> Rejected : empty input
SizeCheck --> Rejected : length > 254
SizeCheck --> LocalPart : length is acceptable

LocalPart : The mailbox name, before the "@"
LocalPart : letters, digits, and . _ % + -
LocalPart --> Rejected : empty, or longer than 64
LocalPart --> Rejected : a character outside the allowed set
LocalPart --> Rejected : a leading or trailing period
LocalPart --> Rejected : two periods in a row
LocalPart --> AtSign : the mailbox name is well formed

AtSign : Exactly one "@"
AtSign --> Rejected : no "@" found
AtSign --> Rejected : a second "@" found
AtSign --> DomainLabel : one "@" found

DomainLabel : One domain label
DomainLabel : letters, digits, and hyphens
DomainLabel --> Rejected : an empty label (two periods in a row)
DomainLabel --> Rejected : a leading or trailing hyphen
DomainLabel --> Rejected : a character outside the allowed set
DomainLabel --> DomainLabel : a period starts the next label
DomainLabel --> TopLevel : the end of the domain is reached

TopLevel : The last label (the top-level domain)
TopLevel --> Rejected : fewer than two characters
TopLevel --> Rejected : a character that is not a letter
TopLevel --> Accepted : at least two letters

Accepted : checkForValidEmailAddress returns ""
Rejected : returns a message, and
Rejected : emailAddressIndexofError points at
Rejected : the character that caused it

Accepted --> [*]
Rejected --> [*]

note right of SizeCheck
  The size check comes first, before any other
  work, so that a very long string dropped into
  the field cannot slow the application down or
  make it fail.  The same pattern is used by the
  UserName recognizer and the password evaluator.
end note

note bottom of Accepted
  What this diagram does NOT test: whether the
  mailbox actually exists.  That cannot be
  determined syntactically and is out of scope
  for TP1.
end note

@enduml
